00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:18.220
Okay, it’s still 2022 and if we think about
image generation, we are not thinking of GANs

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:24.670
anymore, but of diffusion models such as DALL-E
2 or Stable Diffusion, Imagen and so on.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.720
For more details on diffusion models, check
out our previous videos.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.950
Or we are thinking of transformer-based image
generation such as DALL-E 1.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:38.580
The outputs of these models are amazing, but
they come at a computational cost.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.690
Diffusion models take hundreds of sampling
steps – so of inference steps of the same

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:48.610
U-Net neural network – to gradually paint
structure from noise.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.920
So diffusion models take a long time to produce
an image for the end user.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:59.080
Transformer based image generators have two
problems: As transformers usually work with

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:04.590
sets of vectors, one must project the two-dimensional
images into vectors.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:10.090
One should not use too many vectors as a representation
of each image, since the computation time

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:15.500
grows quadratically with the numbers of vectors,
so with the sequence length, due to the self-attention

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:16.500
layers

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:21.340
To generate an image, transformers generate
one such image vector at a time, which takes

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.340
a while.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.530
For more details on generating not images,
but videos with transformers, check out our

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:31.489
previous video on Phenaki that generates endless
videos with transformers.

00:03:31.489 --> 00:03:37.599
But this paper, proposes Paella, which employs
another way to generate images from text without

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:42.780
diffusion, without transformers, but with
just good old convolutional neural networks,

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.120
or CNNs in short.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.060
How does it do it, then?

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:52.189
The authors do the following: They take the
image and use a VQ-GAN to represent the image

00:03:52.189 --> 00:03:54.319
in a lower-dimensional space.

00:03:54.319 --> 00:03:55.870
A VQ-what?

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:59.900
Don’t worry Ms. Coffee Bean, we’ll explain
the VQGAN in a nutshell.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:05.240
The main idea of a VQ-GAN is to take the image,
run it through an encoder neural network,

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:11.090
in this case CNN-based, to represent it in
a lower dimensional space.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:15.240
You are correct to be reminded of the Latent
Diffusion models, because this is a trick

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.519
they employ there too.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:22.009
Then a decoder neural network, also CNN-based
in this case, takes in the lower dimensional

00:04:22.009 --> 00:04:25.060
representation and reconstructs the image.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.540
The encoder and decoder are trained together
in this image reconstruction setting.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.830
Now what about the quantization part?

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:37.580
Well, without quantization, the latent representation
of images would be dense, and all possible

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:42.990
representations are allowed as long as they
are vectors in this lower dimensional space.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:49.290
In this example, our latent space is two dimensional
and the x-es stand each for a different image.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:54.410
But a VQGAN says no: Let’s not learn any
embedding, let’s just map everything to

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:57.160
a learned codebook of a given size.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:03.220
If the encoder says that this picture lands
around here, the quantization step takes the

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:08.220
nearest vector from the learned codebook,
here in red and assigns it as a representation

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.840
for this image, which we call a codeword.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.960
In this way, the decoder does not need to
reconstruct from any vector in the space,

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:21.520
but only from the codebook vectors, which
act like the centroids of the data.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.770
You can think of the VQGAN as of an autoencoder
that also learns to cluster the data with

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:31.449
nearest neighbors.

00:05:31.449 --> 00:05:36.020
In the training procedure of the VQGAN, the
objective is to reconstruct images.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:41.669
To successfully do so, we learn the encoder
weights, the decoder weights and what the

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:44.240
optimal entries of this codebook are.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:55.570
So, for an image in three channels of 256x256,
the authors learn a codeword sized 32x32x256,

00:05:55.570 --> 00:06:02.240
so they represent an image which is a stack
of three larger matrices as a stack of 256

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.750
smaller 32x32 matrices.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:08.300
Now we have a way to represent each image
in a learned codebook, so in a meaningful

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.949
lower dimensional representation.

00:06:10.949 --> 00:06:14.230
How to generate images from text using this
codebook?

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:20.940
The authors take the codeword of an image
and noise out a fraction f of the 256 small

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.440
matrices, so image tokens representing it.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:29.130
Noising means that they are taking another
random codeword from the codebook to replace

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:30.130
it.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:33.020
In other words, they use the centroid of another
image token cluster.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:37.950
Then they send the noised representation to
Paella for denoising.

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:41.699
Paella is CNN-based and not transformer-based
like prior work.

00:06:41.699 --> 00:06:46.050
This has the advantage of being more runtime
efficient and can work with larger inputs,

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:51.310
therefore the compression into the codebook
does not have to be that stark, so important

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.790
details from the image can be kept.

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:59.389
Paella takes the noised representation, the
number of steps and a condition to denoise

00:06:59.389 --> 00:07:01.270
the latent representation.

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:04.800
You have one guess for what this condition
is:

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.050
Of course, it is a text representation, coming
from CLIP embeddings.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:13.040
Of course, it could be anything, like class
labels or semantic segmentation maps.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:18.600
But to do text-conditioned image generation,
the authors take the text, run it through

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:24.341
CLIP’s text branch to get a text representation
and Paella learns through cross-entropy and

00:07:24.341 --> 00:07:32.680
classifier-free guidance to reproduce the
uncorrupted codewords of the image.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:37.640
If you want to know what classifier free guidance
is, check out our previous video.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:42.009
The reconstructed latent space representation
can run through the decoder.

00:07:42.009 --> 00:07:47.060
And so, at the decoder output, we have an
image conditioned on text.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:52.509
During inference, to produce an image from
just text, Paella takes a codeword of just

00:07:52.509 --> 00:07:53.509
noise.

00:07:53.509 --> 00:07:58.500
It could try to denoise everything at once,
but during training, it has never encountered

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.770
the case of total noise in the input, so it
would do quite badly.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:09.860
Therefore, it denoises the codeword in a sequence
of 8 steps (hm, how diffusion-like, but it

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:13.390
is actually more similar with masked language
modelling).

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:19.970
At each step, the authors denoise everything,
but randomly renoise tokens again to give

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:25.900
the model a chance to denoise these based
on the already predicted tokens.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:32.519
If you think this is very much like MaskGIT
in Phenaki, you are right.

00:08:32.519 --> 00:08:38.560
The difference here is that the authors do
not use MASK tokens, but random tokens.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:42.000
Because here at inference we are still in
the latent space, the decoder reconstructs

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.060
the pixel space of the represented image.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.750
This was Paella in a nutshell.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:52.410
Since Paella only contains convolutions and
no transformers and other things, it can do

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:57.839
latent space interpolation, inpaint images
(so imagine what a hole in an image might

00:08:57.839 --> 00:09:00.589
be filled with) or do structural editing.

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:08.480
The authors trained it on 600 million images
from the improved LAION-5B aesthetic (like

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:16.040
StableDiffusion) on 64 NVIDIA A100 for two
weeks – with support from Stability AI,

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.300
for sure.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:21.300
You can judge for yourself what you think
of the generations or look at the author’s

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:26.800
FID metrics, which you know, are not always
aligned to what humans think is a good image.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.510
Maybe these generations are not what you saw
from carefully prompted diffusion models,

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:36.570
but keep in mind that Paella is relatively
small in terms of numbers of parameters compared

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:40.350
to existing diffusion models, need way less
sampling steps

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:44.320
and takes half a second to generate an image.

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:50.089
The authors provide all model weights of Paella,
together with a pytorch-based implementation

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:51.840
in their github repository.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:55.420
They even have a Google Colab and are on Huggingface
spaces.

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:57.470
So do check them out.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:03.130
Now, we are leaving you with Paellas version
of “The cutest coffee bean there is.”

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:04.130
and hope to see you next time!

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:04.132
Okay, bye!

